.policy "ruff-isc001-single-line-implicit-string-concatenation" revision 2
.description "Join string literals that are implicitly concatenated on one line into a single literal; the split adds nothing and may hide a forgotten comma."
.language python
.mode steer
.scope include "**/*"

// Ruff ISC001 (flake8-implicit-str-concat): two string literals side by side on one line, which Python joins
// into one. On one line that is usually a missing comma, or a leftover of a reformat. Any pair counts: plain,
// f-, t- and bytes strings, inside f-string interpolations too; a pair split over lines (ISC002) does not.
// Reported at the first literal of the pair. (Ruff's fix, merging them, is not offered here.)
//   _ = "a" "b" "c"                ← ISC001 at "a" and at "b"
//   _ = f"a {'b' 'c'} e"           ← ISC001 inside the interpolation
//   _ = ("abc"
//        "def")                     not reported: on two lines
#include "python/ast.rr"

.violation ISC001(at: node) code "ISC001"
    "Merge these adjacent literals into one string, escaping any braces or quotes the merge exposes; if they were meant as separate items, add the missing comma instead."
    suppress ruff "ISC001"
ISC001(a) :- kind(cs, "concatenated_string"), child(cs, a, _), kind(a, "string"), next_sibling(a, b), kind(b, "string"),
    end_line(a, l), line(b, l).
